  "response": "<issues>\n- Setting `np.random.seed(seed)` modifies the global random state, which introduces unintended side effects for other parts of the application. A local random state should be used instead.\n- If `target_array` is passed as a pandas Series with a non-default index, assigning it via `df[target_name] = target_array` will result in index misalignment, introducing `NaN` values and corrupting the training data.\n- If `target_name` happens to overlap with a name in `feature_names`, the assignment `df[target_name] = target_array` will silently overwrite one of the feature columns.\n- Adding the target to the feature DataFrame only to immediately separate them in `clf.fit(df[feature_names], df[target_name])` is redundant. It is cleaner and safer to pass the features and target directly to `fit()`.\n</issues>\n<solution>\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\n\ndef task_func(\n    feature_array,\n    target_array,\n    feature_names=[\"f1\", \"f2\", \"f3\", \"f4\", \"f5\"],\n    target_name=\"target\",\n    seed=None,\n):\n    # Use a local RandomState to prevent global side effects if a seed is provided, \n    # but preserve the fallback to global state if seed is None\n    if seed is not None:\n        rng = np.random.RandomState(seed)\n        shuffled_indices = rng.permutation(feature_array.shape[1])\n    else:\n        shuffled_indices = np.random.permutation(feature_array.shape[1])\n        \n    shuffled_array = feature_array[:, shuffled_indices]\n    df = pd.DataFrame(shuffled_array, columns=feature_names)\n    \n    # Train directly to avoid index alignment issues and column overwriting\n    clf = RandomForestClassifier(random_state=seed)\n    clf.fit(df, target_array)\n    \n    return clf\n</solution>",
